  "question": {
    "text": "Let the equation $\\mathrm{x}(\\mathrm{x}+2)(12-\\mathrm{k})=2$ have equal roots. Then the distance of the point $\\left(\\mathrm{k}, \\frac{\\mathrm{k}}{2}\\right)$ from the line $3 x+4 y+5=0$ is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$15$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$5 \\sqrt{3}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$15 \\sqrt{5}$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$12$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned}<br/>& \\left(\\mathrm{x}^2+2 \\mathrm{x}\\right)(12-\\mathrm{k})=2 \\\\\n& \\lambda \\mathrm{x}^2+2 \\lambda \\mathrm{x}-2=0 \\quad \\mathrm{k} \\neq 12 \\text { Let } 12-\\mathrm{k}=\\lambda \\\\\n& \\mathrm{D}=0 \\\\\n& 4 \\lambda^2+8 \\lambda=0 \\\\\n& \\lambda=0 \\text { or } \\lambda=-2 \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow 12-\\mathrm{k}=-2 \\\\\n& \\mathrm{k}=14<br/>\\end{aligned}$<br/>$\\text { So } P\\left(k, \\frac{k}{2}\\right)=(14,7)$<br/>$\\mathrm{d}=\\left|\\frac{3 \\times 14+4 \\times 7+5}{5}\\right|=15$<br/>option (1)"
  }
}